 "name": "Historic Block Public Spaces: Semantic Differential (Sanhe Ancient Town)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the semantic differential (SD) survey in Ding, Wei, Jin, Nie, Zhang, Zhou & Ma (2023), Sustainability 15:2790. Participants first give consent, gender, age group and occupation (design-related discipline or not). They then rate 12 photographs of public spaces in the historical and cultural block of Sanhe Ancient Town (Hefei, China) on the 17 bipolar adjective pairs of the paper's Appendix A, each on a 7-point scale from −3 to +3. The pairs cover material factors (openness, variation, distinctiveness, color, passage, shade, greenery), space atmosphere (aesthetic, uniformity, cleanliness, liveliness, traditional style) and experience (comfort, safety, attractiveness, likeability, overall impression). The photos show inner streets of East, Middle and West Street, the outer waterfront, entrances, an intersection and the assembly square. Analysis as in the paper: mean SD profile per photo and per item, compared between professional and non-professional respondents. Stimuli: the 12 photographs of the paper's Figure 2 (CC BY 4.0). Credit: doi:10.3390/su15032790. The template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) The figure's red map-connector lines and dashed guide lines that crossed some photos were digitally retouched out; a few photos show slight smoothing where a line was removed. (2) The photos are about 700 px wide, as printed in the figure; the paper does not say which photo shows which space, so they are named space01–space12 in the clockwise order of Figure 2. (3) The paper does not give the presentation order; photos are shown one at a time in that fixed order. (4) The eye-tracking experiment (Tobii, 50 participants) is not part of this online template. (5) Item 11 uses \"Deserted – Lively\"; the paper's English appendix says \"Hilarious\", which is a translation slip for the Table 1 pair \"lively – cold and quiet\".",
